    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.panther_similarity - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.panther_similarity.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.panther_similarity()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Find the nodes most similar to a source node by how often they share random\n    paths.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.panther_similarity()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Find the nodes most similar to a source node by how often they share random\n    paths.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.panther_similarity()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Find the nodes most similar to a source node by how often they share random\n  paths.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nFind the nodes most similar to a source node by how often they share random paths.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L37229-L37387){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.panther_similarity(\n    source,\n    k=5,\n    path_length=5,\n    c=0.5,\n    delta=0.1,\n    epsilon=None,\n    weighted=None,\n    seed=None,\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Panther similarity](../assets/graphs/panther-similarity.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Two triangles, a, b, c and x, y, z, joined by the bridge c to x. Panther samples random paths and scores each node by how often it shares a path with the source a (outlined). The k = 3 most similar nodes are in the accent color with their scores as badges: c 0.577 and b 0.462 in a's own triangle, then x 0.346 across the bridge. The card ranks them.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nPanther (Zhang et al., 2015) calls two nodes similar when they often appear on the same\npaths. It samples many short random paths, each starting at a random node and stepping\nto a random neighbor (with probability proportional to the edge weight), and scores each\nnode by the share of paths it shares with `source`. Nodes close to the source in a dense\npart of the graph score highly, so Panther finds the top-$k$ similar nodes on large\nnetworks quickly, without the all-pairs work of `simrank_similarity()`. The scores are\nestimates; pass `seed=` for repeatable results.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[source]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The node to compare against, as an ID or label.\n\n<code>[k]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[5]{.dv}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   How many of the most similar nodes to return.\n\n<code>[path_length]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[5]{.dv}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   The number of steps in each random path ($T$), at least 2.\n\n<code>[c]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [float]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[0.5]{.fl}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   A positive constant that scales the number of sampled paths.\n\n<code>[delta]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [float]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[0.1]{.fl}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   The probability, between 0 and 1, that the scores miss the `epsilon=` error bound.\n\n<code>[epsilon]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [float | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   The error bound on the scores. If `None` (default), $\\sqrt{1 / |E|}$, the value the\n    authors recommend.\n\n<code>[weighted]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [bool | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Whether to use edge weights for the random steps. If `None` (default), follows the\n    graph's `weighted` flag; edges without a weight count as `1.0`.\n\n<code>[seed]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [int | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Random seed for reproducibility.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [dict[int, float]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   Up to `k` node IDs (never `source`) with their scores, from most to least similar\n    (ties in node ID order). A score is the share of the sampled paths that pass\n    through both nodes.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If `source` doesn't exist or is isolated, a parameter is out of range, a weight is\n    negative, or (in a directed graph) a path reaches a node with no out-edges.\n:::\n\n## Warns {.doc-warns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [UserWarning]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If `k` is more than the number of other non-isolated nodes, all of which are\n    returned.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nIsolated nodes are dropped first. The number of sampled paths is\n$R = \\lceil (c / \\varepsilon^2)(\\log_2 \\binom{T + 1}{2} + 1 + \\ln(1/\\delta)) \\rceil$,\nwhich by the authors' sampling bound makes each score an $\\varepsilon$-approximation of\nits expected value with probability at least $1 - \\delta$. Each path of $T$ steps has\n$T + 1$ nodes (hence $\\binom{T + 1}{2}$ pairs of positions) and starts at a uniformly\nrandom node, and a score is the number of paths holding both nodes divided by $R$. The\nsample size follows the fix in NetworkX pull request #8578; NetworkX releases without it\nuse $\\lfloor \\cdot \\rfloor$ and $\\binom{T}{2}$, so they sample slightly fewer paths.\nThis follows NetworkX's `panther_similarity`, except\nthat NetworkX picks the top `k` nodes before dropping the source and so returns `k - 1`\nnodes, while this method returns `k` other nodes. The random paths come from a different\ngenerator, so the scores agree with NetworkX's only statistically.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~panther_vector_similarity`) \n\n:   Similarity of structural role rather than closeness.\n\n[](`~generate_random_paths`) \n\n:   The random paths that the scores count.\n\n[](`~simrank_similarity`) \n\n:   Similarity from random walks that meet.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nPanther samples random paths and scores how often a node shares one with the source. In a\npath of five nodes, the nodes nearest `A` share the most:\n\n::: {#3c1d4d4c .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"D\"), (\"D\", \"E\")]\n)\nlabel = graph.get_label_from_id\n\nscores = graph.panther_similarity(\"A\", k=3, seed=1)\n\n{label(n): round(s, 3) for n, s in scores.items()}\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n{'B': 0.533, 'C': 0.533, 'D': 0.267}\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Close to Hugo\n\n`friends()` links 24 members of two clubs in a made-up town, a chess club and a climbing\nclub, who are friends. The five people who most often share a random path with Hugo are\nhis five friends, Tara among them:\n\n::: {#f77f8713 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nfriends = ec.friends()\nlabel = friends.get_label_from_id\nhugo = friends.panther_similarity(\"Hugo\", seed=1)\n\n{label(n): round(s, 3) for n, s in hugo.items()}\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n{'Oona': 0.122, 'Tara': 0.109, 'Ben': 0.09, 'Jules': 0.083, 'Wes': 0.083}\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nWith `k=` larger, everyone with a score is ranked, and the scores color the network:\n\n::: {#8712b3f7 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nscores = friends.panther_similarity(\"Hugo\", k=23, seed=1)\nfriends = friends.set_node_attrs(\"like_hugo\", scores)\n\nfriends.plot(color_by=\"like_hugo\", label_by=\"label\", seed=8)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-59197dc4:#6593cb;--ect-2dd6b114:#335d94;--ect-191b72ec:#5e8bc3;--ect-ce3c29cd:#5884bc;--ect-8d74738b:#78a7e0;--ect-88fdb3f7:#6b9ad2;--ect-e5626bc2:#80afe8;--ect-975840b8:#86b6ef;--ect-6425850a:#0d366b;--ect-f371a095:#2c558c;--ect-a2be67a8:#d1d5db;--ect-341a3a03:#1a4379;--ect-f8094ac6:#79a8e1;--ect-472663e1:#6d9bd3;--ect-6826a5f9:#608ec6;--ect-9d43aea2:#5480b8;--ect-5ee57d66:#4874ab;--ect-